{"result":"******************************************************************************\n Revised: Sep 30, 2026        Aditya-L1 / (Earth-Sun Lagrange)            -156\n                           https://www.isro.gov.in/Aditya_L1.html\n\n PURPOSE\n  Aditya-L1 is an ISRO spacecraft designed to study the Sun over 5 years from\n  the vantage point of a halo orbit around Sun-Earth Lagrange point 1 (L1) \n  1.5 million km from the Earth in the sunward direction, orbiting that point \n  in space with an irregularly shaped trajectory in a plane roughly \n  perpendicular to the line joining the Earth and the Sun.\n\n BACKGROUND\n  Aditya-L1 was launched 2023-Sep-02 @ 06:20 UTC from Satish Dhawan Space \n  Centre on Sriharikota Island, India on a PSLV-C57 rocket. \n\n  It achieved a staging orbit around the Earth and separated from its fourth\n  stage at 07:27 UTC on September 02, 2023.\n\n  A series of five manuevers set up injection to a transfer orbit to the \n  L1 destination, where it will arrive ~127 days after launch (2024-Jan-7).\n\n  Aditya L1 will study the Sun's corona (visible and near-infrared), Sun's \n  photosphere (soft and hard X-ray), chromosphere (ultraviolet ), solar \n  emissions, solar winds and flares, Coronal Mass Ejections (CMEs), and will \n  carry out round-the-clock imaging of the Sun.\n\n  The payload consists of seven science instruments which include \n  electromagnetic, particle, and magnetic field detectors.\n\n SPACECRAFT DETAILS\n  Launch mass  : 1472 kg\n  Payload mass :  244 kg \n\n SCIENCE PAYLOADS\n  Visible Emission Line Coronagraph (VELC)\n  Solar Ultraviolet Imaging Telescope (SUIT)\n  Solar Low Energy X-ray Spectrometer (SoLEXS)\n  High Energy L1 Orbiting X-ray Spectrometer(HEL1OS)\n  Aditya Solar wind Particle Experiment (ASPEX)\n  Plasma Analyser Package For Aditya (PAPA)\n  Advanced Tri-axial High Resolution Digital Magnetometers\n\n TRAJECTORY: \n  Post-launch trajectory from ISRO. Fit to tracking data through September 27, \n  with a planning prediction thereafter.\n\n  Trajectory name                             Start (TDB)        Stop (TDB)\n  ---------------------------------------- -----------------  -----------------\n  Aditya-L1_merged (merge of 58 files)     2023-Sep-02 07:25  2027-Jan-21 03:00\n*******************************************************************************\n\n\n*******************************************************************************\nEphemeris / API_USER Tue Oct 27 09:16:22 2026 Pasadena, USA      / Horizons\n*******************************************************************************\nTarget body name: Aditya-L1 (-156)                {source: Aditya-L1_merged}\nCenter body name: Earth (399)                     {source: Aditya-L1_merged}\nCenter-site name: BODY CENTER\n*******************************************************************************\nStart time      : A.D. 2023-Sep-02 07:24:30.0000 UT \nStop  time      : A.D. 2023-Sep-03 06:20:00.0000 UT \nStep-size       : 10 minutes\n*******************************************************************************\nCenter geodetic : 0.0, 0.0, 0.0                   {E-lon(deg),Lat(deg),Alt(km)}\nCenter cylindric: 0.0, 0.0, 0.0                   {E-lon(deg),Dxy(km),Dz(km)}\nCenter radii    : 6378.137, 6378.137, 6356.752 km {Equator_a, b, pole_c}\nOutput units    : KM-S\nCalendar mode   : Mixed Julian/Gregorian\nOutput type     : GEOMETRIC cartesian states\nOutput format   : 2 (position and velocity)\nEOP file        : eop.261026.p270122\nEOP coverage    : DATA-BASED 1962-JAN-20 TO 2026-OCT-26. PREDICTS-> 2027-JAN-21\nReference frame : ICRF\n*******************************************************************************\n            JDUT ,            Calendar Date (UT ),                      X,                      Y,                      Z,                     VX,                     VY,                     VZ,\n***********************************************************************************************